card.statement:
Let $\mathbb F$ be a field or skew field with involution $($possibly, the identity if $F$ is a field$)$, $V$ be a right vector space over $\mathbb F$, and $U$ be its subspace. Let ${\cal G}$ be one of sesquilinear forms

\begin{equation}
U\times V\rightarrow {\mathbb
F}, \qquad (V/U)\times
V\rightarrow {\mathbb F}.
\end{equation}

(a) There exists a basis $e_1,\dots,e_n$ of\/ $V$ satisfying <<ref:0.21>>, in which the matrix <<ref:0.22>> of ${\cal G}$ is a block-direct sum of a $p$-by-$p$ matrix

\begin{equation}
[K|0_{p0}],\qquad \text{$K$ is
nonsingular,}
\end{equation}

and matrices of the form

\begin{equation}
[J_q(0)|0_{q0}]\ \ (q\ge 1),
\qquad
[J_q(0)|E_q]\ \ (q\ge 0),
\end{equation}

in which

\begin{equation}
E_q:=\begin{bmatrix} \,0\,\\
\vdots\\ 0\\ 1
\end{bmatrix}\ \text{if }q\ge 1,
\qquad
E_0:=0_{01}
\end{equation}

$($the summands <<ref:0.10>> or <<ref:0.11>> may be absent$)$. The block $K$ is determined by ${\cal G}$ uniquely up to * congruence, and the summands of the form <<ref:0.11>> are determined by ${\cal G}$ uniquely up to permutation.

(b) If\/ $\mathbb F=\mathbb C$, then one can replace in this direct sum the summand <<ref:0.10>> by

\begin{equation*}
[K_1|0_{p_10}]\uplus\dots\uplus
[K_s|0_{p_s0}],
\end{equation*}

where $K_1\oplus\dots\oplus K_s$ is the canonical form of $K$ defined in Theorem <<ref:bilin>> and each $K_i$ is $p_i$-by-$p_i$. The obtained block-direct sum is determined by ${\cal G}$ uniquely up to permutation of summands, and so it is a canonical matrix of the sesquilinear $($in particular, bilinear) form ${\cal
G}$.
